Well, CLL does not list a selma'o CMENE in the list of all the selma'o. It =
does use selma'o style reference ("CMENE") in two rules, one of them merely=
 the phonological definition of what are now called cmevla, the other defin=
ing the multi-cmevla cmene, which are called cmene=A0 and are clearly not i=
n CMENE.=A0 None of the other things that are names, e.g., {la tsani} are c=
alled cmene either and even regular cmevla and cmevla strings are called na=
mes throughout most of the text (other than the section on the phonological=
 definition).=A0 So, nobody is really sticking to CLL too closely.=A0 A nom=
enclature conference might come in handy.=A0 Personally, I like "names".




